[{"input":"# Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain what an algorithm is.\n- Understand the principles of computational thinking.\n- Read and write simple pseudocode.\n- Interpret basic flowcharts.\n- Understand variables and data types.\n- Explain expressions and assignments.\n- Understand conditions and loops.\n- Explain the purpose of functions.\n- Recognize common data structures.\n- Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.\n\nHowever, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:\n\n> How do we describe a solution to a problem?\n\nComputer science is not primarily about programming languages.\n\nIt is about:\n- Solving problems\n- Organizing information\n- Designing processes\n\nProgramming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.\n\nThe first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.\n\n## What Is an Algorithm?\n\nAn algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.\n\nAn algorithm must be:\n- Precise\n- Unambiguous\n- Executable\n- Finite\n\n### Everyday Examples\n\nMany tasks can be described as algorithms.\n\n#### Making Coffee\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Finding a Name in a Contact List\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Important Observation\n\nAlgorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.\n\n## Computational Thinking\n\nComputational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.\n\n### Decomposition\n\nBreak a large problem into smaller parts.\n\n#### Example\n\nBuilding a website can be divided into:\n- Design\n- Content\n- Programming\n- Testing\n- Deployment\n\nSmaller problems are usually easier to solve.\n\n### Pattern Recognition\n\nIdentify similarities between problems.\n\n#### Example\n\nMany online stores use similar functions:\n- Product search\n- Shopping cart\n- Payment system\n\nRecognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.\n\n### Abstraction\n\nFocus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.\n\n#### Example\n\nA metro map shows:\n- Stations\n- Connections\n\nbut not:\n- Buildings\n- Trees\n- Street furniture\n\nAbstraction simplifies understanding.\n\n### Algorithm Design\n\nDevelop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.","output":"","result":"# Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Explain what an algorithm is.\n- Understand the principles of computational thinking.\n- Read and write simple pseudocode.\n- Interpret basic flowcharts.\n- Understand variables and data types.\n- Explain expressions and assignments.\n- Understand conditions and loops.\n- Explain the purpose of functions.\n- Recognize common data structures.\n- Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.\n\nHowever, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:\n\n> How do we describe a solution to a problem?\n\nComputer science is not primarily about programming languages.\n\nIt is about:\n- Solving problems\n- Organizing information\n- Designing processes\n\nProgramming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.\n\nThe first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.\n\n## What Is an Algorithm?\n\nAn algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.\n\nAn algorithm must be:\n- Precise\n- Unambiguous\n- Executable\n- Finite\n\n### Everyday Examples\n\nMany tasks can be described as algorithms.\n\n#### Making Coffee\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Finding a Name in a Contact List\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Important Observation\n\nAlgorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.\n\n## Computational Thinking\n\nComputational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.\n\n### Decomposition\n\nBreak a large problem into smaller parts.\n\n#### Example\n\nBuilding a website can be divided into:\n- Design\n- Content\n- Programming\n- Testing\n- Deployment\n\nSmaller problems are usually easier to solve.\n\n### Pattern Recognition\n\nIdentify similarities between problems.\n\n#### Example\n\nMany online stores use similar functions:\n- Product search\n- Shopping cart\n- Payment system\n\nRecognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.\n\n### Abstraction\n\nFocus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.\n\n#### Example\n\nA metro map shows:\n- Stations\n- Connections\n\nbut not:\n- Buildings\n- Trees\n- Street furniture\n\nAbstraction simplifies understanding.\n\n### Algorithm Design\n\nDevelop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-lecture-6-algorithms-and-programming-fundamentals\">\n<h1>Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain what an algorithm is.</li>\n<li>Understand the principles of computational thinking.</li>\n<li>Read and write simple pseudocode.</li>\n<li>Interpret basic flowcharts.</li>\n<li>Understand variables and data types.</li>\n<li>Explain expressions and assignments.</li>\n<li>Understand conditions and loops.</li>\n<li>Explain the purpose of functions.</li>\n<li>Recognize common data structures.</li>\n<li>Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>In the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.</p>\n<p>However, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we describe a solution to a problem?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Computer science is not primarily about programming languages.</p>\n<p>It is about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solving problems</li>\n<li>Organizing information</li>\n<li>Designing processes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Programming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.</p>\n<p>The first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-what-is-an-algorithm\">\n<h1>What Is an Algorithm?</h1>\n<p>An algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.</p>\n<p>An algorithm must be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Precise</li>\n<li>Unambiguous</li>\n<li>Executable</li>\n<li>Finite</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-everyday-examples\">\n<h1>Everyday Examples</h1>\n<p>Many tasks can be described as algorithms.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-making-coffee\">\n<h1>Making Coffee</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-finding-a-name-in-a-contact-list\">\n<h1>Finding a Name in a Contact List</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-important-observation\">\n<h1>Important Observation</h1>\n<p>Algorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-computational-thinking\">\n<h1>Computational Thinking</h1>\n<p>Computational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-decomposition\">\n<h1>Decomposition</h1>\n<p>Break a large problem into smaller parts.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Building a website can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Design</li>\n<li>Content</li>\n<li>Programming</li>\n<li>Testing</li>\n<li>Deployment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Smaller problems are usually easier to solve.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-pattern-recognition\">\n<h1>Pattern Recognition</h1>\n<p>Identify similarities between problems.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Many online stores use similar functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Product search</li>\n<li>Shopping cart</li>\n<li>Payment system</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-abstraction\">\n<h1>Abstraction</h1>\n<p>Focus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example-3\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>A metro map shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stations</li>\n<li>Connections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>but not:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buildings</li>\n<li>Trees</li>\n<li>Street furniture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Abstraction simplifies understanding.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-algorithm-design\">\n<h1>Algorithm Design</h1>\n<p>Develop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Pseudocode\n\nBefore writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.\n\n### Example\n\nProblem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\nPseudocode is:\n- Easy to read\n- Language-independent\n- Useful for planning solutions\n\n### Another Example\n\nDetermine whether someone can vote.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```","output":"","result":"## Pseudocode\n\nBefore writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.\n\n### Example\n\nProblem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\nPseudocode is:\n- Easy to read\n- Language-independent\n- Useful for planning solutions\n\n### Another Example\n\nDetermine whether someone can vote.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-pseudocode\">\n<h1>Pseudocode</h1>\n<p>Before writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Problem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-advantages\">\n<h1>Advantages</h1>\n<p>Pseudocode is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Easy to read</li>\n<li>Language-independent</li>\n<li>Useful for planning solutions</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-another-example\">\n<h1>Another Example</h1>\n<p>Determine whether someone can vote.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ age\n\nIF age &gt;= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Flowcharts\n\nFlowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.\n\n### Common Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Oval | Start / End |\n| Rectangle | Process |\n| Diamond | Decision |\n| Parallelogram | Input / Output |\n\n### Example Flow\n\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Why Use Flowcharts? \n\nFlowcharts help: \n\n- Visualize algorithms \n\n- Communicate ideas \n\n- Identify mistakes ","output":"","result":"## Flowcharts\n\nFlowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.\n\n### Common Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Oval | Start / End |\n| Rectangle | Process |\n| Diamond | Decision |\n| Parallelogram | Input / Output |\n\n### Example Flow\n\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Why Use Flowcharts? \n\nFlowcharts help: \n\n- Visualize algorithms \n\n- Communicate ideas \n\n- Identify mistakes ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-flowcharts\">\n<h1>Flowcharts</h1>\n<p>Flowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-common-symbols\">\n<h1>Common Symbols</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Oval</td>\n<td>Start / End</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rectangle</td>\n<td>Process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diamond</td>\n<td>Decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parallelogram</td>\n<td>Input / Output</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-example-flow\">\n<h1>Example Flow</h1>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png\" alt=\"\">\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-why-use-flowcharts\">\n<h1>Why Use Flowcharts?</h1>\n<p>Flowcharts help:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Visualize algorithms</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Communicate ideas</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Identify mistakes</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Variables\n\nPrograms often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariable: `age`\n\nValue: `20`\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a variable as a labeled box.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nThe value can later change.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nThe same variable now contains a different value.\n\n## Data Types\n\nDifferent kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.\n\n### Integer\nWhole numbers.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Real Number\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Text (String)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Boolean\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nBooleans are heavily used in decisions.\n\n## Expressions and Assignment\n\nPrograms frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Assignment\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nResult: `121`","output":"","result":"## Variables\n\nPrograms often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariable: `age`\n\nValue: `20`\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a variable as a labeled box.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nThe value can later change.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nThe same variable now contains a different value.\n\n## Data Types\n\nDifferent kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.\n\n### Integer\nWhole numbers.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Real Number\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Text (String)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Boolean\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nBooleans are heavily used in decisions.\n\n## Expressions and Assignment\n\nPrograms frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Assignment\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nResult: `121`","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-variables\">\n<h1>Variables</h1>\n<p>Programs often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">age = 20</pre>\n<p>Variable: <code>age</code></p>\n<p>Value: <code>20</code></p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Think of a variable as a labeled box.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Box label: age\nContents: 20</pre>\n<p>The value can later change.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">age = 20\n\nage = 21</pre>\n<p>The same variable now contains a different value.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-data-types\">\n<h1>Data Types</h1>\n<p>Different kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-integer\">\n<h1>Integer</h1>\n<p>Whole numbers.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">5\n-3\n42</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-real-number\">\n<h1>Real Number</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">3.14\n12.5\n0.01</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-text-string\">\n<h1>Text (String)</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-boolean\">\n<h1>Boolean</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">TRUE\nFALSE</pre>\n<p>Booleans are heavily used in decisions.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-expressions-and-assignment\">\n<h1>Expressions and Assignment</h1>\n<p>Programs frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">5 + 3</pre>\n<p>Result:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">8</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-assignment\">\n<h1>Assignment</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">total = 5 + 3</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">price = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax</pre>\n<p>Result: <code>121</code></p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Input and Output\n\nPrograms typically interact with users.\n\n### Input\n- Name\n- Age\n- Password\n- Temperature\n\n### Output\n- Messages\n- Results\n- Reports\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\nPrograms often make decisions.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Complete Example\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Comparison Operators\n\n| Operator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| = | Equal |\n| ≠ | Not equal |\n| > | Greater than |\n| < | Less than |\n| >= | Greater than or equal |\n| <= | Less than or equal |\n\n## Loops\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```","output":"","result":"## Input and Output\n\nPrograms typically interact with users.\n\n### Input\n- Name\n- Age\n- Password\n- Temperature\n\n### Output\n- Messages\n- Results\n- Reports\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\nPrograms often make decisions.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Complete Example\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Comparison Operators\n\n| Operator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| = | Equal |\n| ≠ | Not equal |\n| > | Greater than |\n| < | Less than |\n| >= | Greater than or equal |\n| <= | Less than or equal |\n\n## Loops\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-input-and-output\">\n<h1>Input and Output</h1>\n<p>Programs typically interact with users.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-input\">\n<h1>Input</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Name</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Password</li>\n<li>Temperature</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-output\">\n<h1>Output</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Messages</li>\n<li>Results</li>\n<li>Reports</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-conditions\">\n<h1>Conditions</h1>\n<p>Programs often make decisions.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">IF temperature &gt; 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-complete-example\">\n<h1>Complete Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">IF age &gt;= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-comparison-operators\">\n<h1>Comparison Operators</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>=</td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>≠</td>\n<td>Not equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;</td>\n<td>Greater than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;</td>\n<td>Less than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;=</td>\n<td>Greater than or equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;=</td>\n<td>Less than or equal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-loops\">\n<h1>Loops</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1\n2\n3\n4\n5</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Functions\n\nA function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCalling:\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Parameters and Return Values\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCall:\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Arrays\n\nAn array stores multiple values of the same type.\n\n### Lists\n\nLists are similar to arrays but more flexible.\n\n### Stacks\n\nLast In, First Out (LIFO).\n\n### Queues\n\nFirst In, First Out (FIFO).\n\n### Why Data Structures Matter\n\nChoosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.\n\n## Algorithm Efficiency\n\nTwo algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:\n- Time\n- Memory\n\nA major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.","output":"","result":"## Functions\n\nA function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCalling:\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Parameters and Return Values\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCall:\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Arrays\n\nAn array stores multiple values of the same type.\n\n### Lists\n\nLists are similar to arrays but more flexible.\n\n### Stacks\n\nLast In, First Out (LIFO).\n\n### Queues\n\nFirst In, First Out (FIFO).\n\n### Why Data Structures Matter\n\nChoosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.\n\n## Algorithm Efficiency\n\nTwo algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:\n- Time\n- Memory\n\nA major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-functions\">\n<h1>Functions</h1>\n<p>A function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION</pre>\n<p>Calling:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">greet()</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Hello</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-parameters-and-return-values\">\n<h1>Parameters and Return Values</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION</pre>\n<p>Call:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">square(4)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">16</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-data-structures\">\n<h1>Data Structures</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-arrays\">\n<h1>Arrays</h1>\n<p>An array stores multiple values of the same type.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-lists\">\n<h1>Lists</h1>\n<p>Lists are similar to arrays but more flexible.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-stacks\">\n<h1>Stacks</h1>\n<p>Last In, First Out (LIFO).</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-queues\">\n<h1>Queues</h1>\n<p>First In, First Out (FIFO).</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-why-data-structures-matter\">\n<h1>Why Data Structures Matter</h1>\n<p>Choosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-algorithm-efficiency\">\n<h1>Algorithm Efficiency</h1>\n<p>Two algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Time</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Summary\n\n- Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.\n- Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.\n- Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.\n- Variables store information.\n- Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.\n- Conditions allow programs to make decisions.\n- Loops allow programs to repeat actions.\n- Functions support modular and reusable software.\n- Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.\n- Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Algorithm | Sequence of steps solving a problem |\n| Computational Thinking | Structured approach to problem solving |\n| Pseudocode | Informal description of an algorithm |\n| Flowchart | Graphical representation of an algorithm |\n| Variable | Named storage location |\n| Data Type | Category of data values |\n| Expression | Computation producing a value |\n| Assignment | Storing a value in a variable |\n| Condition | Decision-making construct |\n| Loop | Repetition construct |\n| Function | Reusable block of instructions |\n| Parameter | Input to a function |\n| Return Value | Output from a function |\n| Array | Ordered collection of values |\n| List | Flexible collection of values |\n| Stack | Last-In First-Out structure |\n| Queue | First-In First-Out structure |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What are the four components of computational thinking?\n- Why is pseudocode useful?\n- What is the purpose of a flowchart?\n- What is a variable?\n- Give examples of four common data types.\n- What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?\n- How do conditions influence program execution?\n- Why are loops important?\n- What are the advantages of functions?\n- What is the difference between a stack and a queue?\n- Why do data structures matter?\n- Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 7\n\nWe now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study the **Operating System**, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.","output":"","result":"## Summary\n\n- Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.\n- Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.\n- Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.\n- Variables store information.\n- Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.\n- Conditions allow programs to make decisions.\n- Loops allow programs to repeat actions.\n- Functions support modular and reusable software.\n- Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.\n- Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Algorithm | Sequence of steps solving a problem |\n| Computational Thinking | Structured approach to problem solving |\n| Pseudocode | Informal description of an algorithm |\n| Flowchart | Graphical representation of an algorithm |\n| Variable | Named storage location |\n| Data Type | Category of data values |\n| Expression | Computation producing a value |\n| Assignment | Storing a value in a variable |\n| Condition | Decision-making construct |\n| Loop | Repetition construct |\n| Function | Reusable block of instructions |\n| Parameter | Input to a function |\n| Return Value | Output from a function |\n| Array | Ordered collection of values |\n| List | Flexible collection of values |\n| Stack | Last-In First-Out structure |\n| Queue | First-In First-Out structure |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What are the four components of computational thinking?\n- Why is pseudocode useful?\n- What is the purpose of a flowchart?\n- What is a variable?\n- Give examples of four common data types.\n- What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?\n- How do conditions influence program execution?\n- Why are loops important?\n- What are the advantages of functions?\n- What is the difference between a stack and a queue?\n- Why do data structures matter?\n- Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 7\n\nWe now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study the **Operating System**, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.</li>\n<li>Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.</li>\n<li>Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.</li>\n<li>Variables store information.</li>\n<li>Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.</li>\n<li>Conditions allow programs to make decisions.</li>\n<li>Loops allow programs to repeat actions.</li>\n<li>Functions support modular and reusable software.</li>\n<li>Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.</li>\n<li>Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithm</td>\n<td>Sequence of steps solving a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computational Thinking</td>\n<td>Structured approach to problem solving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pseudocode</td>\n<td>Informal description of an algorithm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flowchart</td>\n<td>Graphical representation of an algorithm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable</td>\n<td>Named storage location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data Type</td>\n<td>Category of data values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expression</td>\n<td>Computation producing a value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assignment</td>\n<td>Storing a value in a variable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Condition</td>\n<td>Decision-making construct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loop</td>\n<td>Repetition construct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>Reusable block of instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameter</td>\n<td>Input to a function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Return Value</td>\n<td>Output from a function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Array</td>\n<td>Ordered collection of values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>List</td>\n<td>Flexible collection of values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack</td>\n<td>Last-In First-Out structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue</td>\n<td>First-In First-Out structure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>What is an algorithm?</li>\n<li>What are the four components of computational thinking?</li>\n<li>Why is pseudocode useful?</li>\n<li>What is the purpose of a flowchart?</li>\n<li>What is a variable?</li>\n<li>Give examples of four common data types.</li>\n<li>What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?</li>\n<li>How do conditions influence program execution?</li>\n<li>Why are loops important?</li>\n<li>What are the advantages of functions?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between a stack and a queue?</li>\n<li>Why do data structures matter?</li>\n<li>Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-transition-to-lecture-7\">\n<h1>Transition to Lecture 7</h1>\n<p>We now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.</p>\n<p>In the next lecture, we will study the <strong>Operating System</strong>, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]